[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### One provably-equivalent mutant
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Changing `addss(B.r, A.r)` to `addss(A.r, B.r)` in `c2CircletoCircle` survives,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and this is **not** a test gap. Float addition is commutative for every non-NaN
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m input (including `inf + -inf` → the same QNaN-indefinite either way, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0 + -0` → `+0` either way), so the two orders can only differ in the NaN
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *payload*. That payload is consumed solely by `d2 < r2`, which is false for any
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m NaN regardless of payload, and `r2` is never returned. The difference is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m therefore unobservable through the public ABI. The order in `src/lib.rs` still
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m matches the C's emitted `addss %xmm1,%xmm0` (dst = `B.r`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## What the C actually does that the Rust reproduces
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Confirmed by disassembling `c_src/build/CMakeFiles/*/src/lib.c.o` rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m inferred:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `c2Dot` pins `mulss dst=a.x,src=b.x`, `mulss dst=b.y,src=a.y`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `addss dst=(b.y*a.y),src=(a.x*b.x)`. SSE two-operand scalar arithmetic resolves
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   NaNs by preferring **dst**, so this order decides which payload survives.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `c2CircletoCircle` emits `addss %xmm1,%xmm0` with dst = `B.r`, src = `A.r`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `c2Sub` emits `subss dst=a.x,src=b.x`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `c2Maxv`/`c2Minv` use `comiss`+`jbe`, i.e. genuine `a>b ? a : b` — on an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   unordered compare the branch is taken and the **second** operand wins. This
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   differs from `f32::max`/`f32::min`, which is why the Rust uses explicit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   comparisons.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `C2_TYPE` is passed as a 4-byte `int` (`cmpl $0x0,-0xc(%rbp)`), and each
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `switch` arm dereferences its operands **inside the taken case only** — which is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   what makes a null pointer well-defined on the rejecting arms.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `collided`'s `AABB × CIRCLE` arm calls
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `c2CircletoAABB(*(c2Circle*)B, *(c2AABB*)A)` — the operands are swapped
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   relative to the parameter names. Reproduced verbatim, and `cfg_row27` asserts
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the swap is still there.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Behaviours that look like bugs but are the C's ground truth, and are pinned by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m tests rather than "fixed":
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Touching shapes (`d2 == r2`, `A.max.x == B.min.x`) count as **colliding** for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   AABB/AABB and **not colliding** for the circle predicates, because both use a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   strict `<` on different sides.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - A NaN in any AABB component makes every `<` false, so `c2AABBtoAABB` reports a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   **collision**.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Negative radii still produce a positive `r2`, so `c2CircletoCircle` can report a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   collision for them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Inverted boxes (`min > max`) are never normalised.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - A zero-radius circle at a box centre does **not** collide (`0 < 0` is false).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Known non-goals
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `collided` with a **valid** tag and a null/unmapped pointer dereferences
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unconditionally in the C — undefined behaviour, not a defined rejection. There is
